The simplest way to think about it:

> **Temperature changes how much you favor unlikely tokens. Top-P limits which tokens are even allowed to compete.**

### Example

Suppose the model is choosing the next word after:

> "The movie was..."

It calculates probabilities:

| Token       | Probability |
| ----------- | ----------: |
| great       |         50% |
| good        |         25% |
| interesting |         12% |
| terrible    |          6% |
| boring      |          4% |
| confusing   |          3% |

### Temperature

Temperature modifies the **probability distribution**.

**Low temperature:**

```text
great      ████████████████████
good       ████████
interesting ██
terrible   ▏
```

The model strongly prefers the most likely answer.

**High temperature:**

```text
great      ████████████
good       ███████
interesting █████
terrible   ███
boring     ██
confusing  ██
```

Less-likely options get a better chance.

So:

> **Temperature = how "risky" the sampling is.**

---

### Top-P

Top-P works differently.

Suppose:

```text
great        50%
good         25%
interesting  12%
terrible      6%
boring        4%
confusing     3%
```

Set:

```text
top_p = 0.90
```

The model takes the smallest group whose cumulative probability reaches ~90%:

```text
great        50%
good         25%   → 75%
interesting  12%   → 87%
terrible      6%   → 93%  ← stop
```

So only these four candidates participate:

```text
great
good
interesting
terrible
```

`boring` and `confusing` are effectively removed from the sampling pool for that step.

So:

> **Top-P = how wide a probability pool the model is allowed to sample from.**

---

## The key difference

Think of a race.

**Temperature:**

> "Change the odds of the runners."

**Top-P:**

> "Decide which runners are allowed into the race."

That's the cleanest mental model.

---

### Temperature vs Top-P

|                       | Temperature                  | Top-P                     |
| --------------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Changes probabilities | ✅                            | Not directly              |
| Removes candidates    | ❌                            | ✅                         |
| Controls randomness   | ✅                            | ✅                         |
| Main idea             | Change probability sharpness | Restrict probability mass |
| Typical use           | More/less deterministic      | Limit unlikely choices    |

### One subtle but important point

They're **not independent alternatives**.

You can use both:

```text
Temperature → reshape probabilities
                    ↓
Top-P → select probability mass
                    ↓
Sample next token
```

But in practice, you generally don't want to aggressively tune both simultaneously because it becomes harder to understand which parameter caused the behavior.

### For your AI engineering mental model

Remember:

```text
Temperature = "How adventurous?"

Top-P       = "How many plausible choices are we considering?"
```

And one more important distinction:

**Neither makes the model more intelligent.** They change **sampling behavior** after the model has produced its probability distribution.
